WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.950 --> 00:00:04.610
LINDSAY: Good evening, everyone.

00:00:04.610 --> 00:00:08.309
Welcome to tonight's Council on Foreign Relations
meeting.

00:00:08.309 --> 00:00:13.910
I am Jim Lindsay, senior vice president and
director of studies here at the Council on

00:00:13.910 --> 00:00:14.910
Foreign Relations.

00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:20.900
I also want to welcome everyone who's joining
us via the wonders of the Internet as we livestream

00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:24.640
tonight's lively and timely discussion.

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:29.130
It is my great pleasure and honor to introduce
tonight's guests.

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:34.550
I think you're going to learn they are both
terrific talents, and I'm very proud to be

00:00:34.550 --> 00:00:35.550
their colleague.

00:00:35.550 --> 00:00:39.350
First, to my immediate right is Ambassador
Robert Blackwill.

00:00:39.350 --> 00:00:43.990
He is the Henry A. Kissinger Senior Fellow
for U.S. Foreign Policy.

00:00:43.990 --> 00:00:50.161
Now, Bob has had an illustrious career in
public service, having held numerous and will

00:00:50.161 --> 00:00:53.840
say distinguished foreign policy posts over
the years.

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:57.700
I am-beg my pardon, Bob-not going to go through
all of the posts you've had.

00:00:57.700 --> 00:00:59.950
Let me just hit a couple of the highlights.

00:00:59.950 --> 00:01:03.650
Bob has served for-or under five presidents.

00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:09.570
He was U.S. ambassador to India from 2001
to 2003.

00:01:09.570 --> 00:01:14.179
He did multiple stints on the staff of the
National Security Council, including as deputy

00:01:14.179 --> 00:01:19.340
assistant to the president and deputy national
security adviser for strategic planning under

00:01:19.340 --> 00:01:22.039
George W. Bush.

00:01:22.039 --> 00:01:26.579
Between his government stints, Bob was on
the faculty at Harvard's Kennedy School of

00:01:26.579 --> 00:01:28.780
Government for a dozen years.

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:29.780
He wrote a lot.

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:37.380
By my count, he has written, co-written, co-edited
10 books, one of them being the best-selling

00:01:37.380 --> 00:01:42.759
book, with Graham Allison, "Lee Kuan Yew:
The Grand Master's Insights on China, the

00:01:42.759 --> 00:01:44.770
United States, and the World."

00:01:44.770 --> 00:01:51.679
And just this year the Indian government honored
Bob with the 2016 Padma Bhushan Award for

00:01:51.679 --> 00:01:54.389
distinguished service of a high order.

00:01:54.389 --> 00:01:56.520
So, congratulations, Bob.

00:01:56.520 --> 00:01:59.060
Our other guest is Jennifer Harris.

00:01:59.060 --> 00:02:02.070
Jen is a senior fellow here at the Council.

00:02:02.070 --> 00:02:07.729
Before joining the David Rockefeller Studies
Program, Jen served on the staff of the National

00:02:07.729 --> 00:02:13.130
Intelligence Council and on the policy planning
staff of the U.S. State Department, where

00:02:13.130 --> 00:02:18.910
she was a lead architect of Secretary Clinton's
Economic Statecraft agenda.

00:02:18.910 --> 00:02:23.340
Jen is both a Truman and a Rhodes Scholar,
quite an accomplishment.

00:02:23.340 --> 00:02:29.079
And tonight, however, we're not here to sing
their past praises; we're here to talk about

00:02:29.079 --> 00:02:36.840
the publication of their terrific new book,
"War by Other Means: Geoeconomics and Statecraft."

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:45.860
So please join me in welcoming 
Bob and Jen.

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:46.860
(Applause.)

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:47.860
Congratulations, Bob, congratulations, Jen,
on the publication of the book.

00:02:47.860 --> 00:02:48.860
BLACKWILL: Thank you.

00:02:48.860 --> 00:02:53.040
LINDSAY: I will note that The Weekly Standard
called it "readable and lucid"-(laughter)-and-

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:55.220
BLACKWILL: It's better than the alternative.

00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:56.220
(Laughter.)

00:02:56.220 --> 00:02:57.220
LINDSAY: Oh, it's-I'd take it.

00:02:57.220 --> 00:02:58.220
That's pretty good.

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:03.310
And a reviewer in the Indian Express was so
taken with the book that he urged Indian diplomats

00:03:03.310 --> 00:03:08.540
and strategists to read it because it was
relevant to what they do.

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:10.129
Let's begin with the basics.

00:03:10.129 --> 00:03:15.969
Looking at the subtitle of the book, it contains
a word that many people either may not be

00:03:15.969 --> 00:03:21.290
familiar with, or have heard in a variety
of different contexts, and that's the word

00:03:21.290 --> 00:03:22.290
"geoeconomics."

00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:26.120
So I'm going to turn to you first, Bob: What
do you mean by "geoeconomics"?

00:03:26.120 --> 00:03:32.610
BLACKWILL: Well, that turned out to be a question
that Jen and I worked hard on in the early

00:03:32.610 --> 00:03:35.540
stages of our research.

00:03:35.540 --> 00:03:44.870
If you, to our surprise, look in the literature
exhaustively, you find the constant use of

00:03:44.870 --> 00:03:52.719
the term without any definition, and people,
scholars, and public policy practitioners

00:03:52.719 --> 00:03:58.490
using the term without ever defining what
they think it is.

00:03:58.490 --> 00:04:06.430
So we thought hard about it, and came up with
the definition which we think distinguishes

00:04:06.430 --> 00:04:17.820
this particular discipline from others, which
is that geoeconomics is using economic tools

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:21.180
for geopolitical purposes.

00:04:21.180 --> 00:04:28.600
So it's not using economic tools for economic
purposes, although those are fine, notable

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:29.740
objectives.

00:04:29.740 --> 00:04:37.440
It's using these economic tools to advance
a government, a nation's geopolitical interests.

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:47.700
And the book is organized around that definition,
and we look at which countries are skillful

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:56.110
at using those instruments for geopolitical
objectives and which ones are less apt.

00:04:56.110 --> 00:05:05.590
LINDSAY: So geoeconomics is a subset of geopolitics,
rather than something different from geopolitics?

00:05:05.590 --> 00:05:08.430
BLACKWILL: I would say it's parallel to geopolitics.

00:05:08.430 --> 00:05:12.700
Most-and maybe Jen would chime in; we'll see
if we even agree.

00:05:12.700 --> 00:05:19.110
But if you look at geopolitics and you go
back to the 19th century and Mackinder and

00:05:19.110 --> 00:05:30.130
all of that, you discover that it has a very
strong geographic orientation, and a geographic

00:05:30.130 --> 00:05:34.900
orientation based on security, OK?

00:05:34.900 --> 00:05:41.110
And this is a different notion, both because
technology of course has erased many of those

00:05:41.110 --> 00:05:51.770
boundaries, but also the great strategists
from Sun Tzu forward don't usually talk about

00:05:51.770 --> 00:05:59.920
economics as a means to geopolitical objectives.

00:05:59.920 --> 00:06:08.400
And without giving you the punchline, the
United States for most of its history understood

00:06:08.400 --> 00:06:15.420
the utility of these economic instruments
for geopolitical purposes, from the Louisiana

00:06:15.420 --> 00:06:23.200
Purchase to the Marshall Plan-both of which,
of course, were dominated by geopolitical

00:06:23.200 --> 00:06:32.500
considerations-but in more recent decades
have forgotten that historical legacy.

00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:34.700
LINDSAY: Did you want to jump in here, Jen?

00:06:34.700 --> 00:06:35.880
HARRIS: Sure.

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:41.870
In practice, a lot of people know what geopolitics
looks like when they see it.

00:06:41.870 --> 00:06:46.680
It's more or less the way in which states
are struggling for control over territory,

00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:53.030
often by reference to a set of geographic
factors, right: population, military, including

00:06:53.030 --> 00:06:54.710
certain economic factors.

00:06:54.710 --> 00:06:59.850
But where-what we're really trying to describe
is how states are pursuing those same aims,

00:06:59.850 --> 00:07:05.040
but rather than reaching for traditional diplomatic
or military tools, they are reaching for a

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:06.800
whole host of economic tools.

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:12.730
And this can take the form of everything from
trade and investment policies that are wielded

00:07:12.730 --> 00:07:18.760
more, again, in pursuit of certain geopolitical
aims; or certain aspects of the cyber domain,

00:07:18.760 --> 00:07:22.510
the way in which countries are acting-
LINDSAY: So, for you, cyber as part of geoeconomics.

00:07:22.510 --> 00:07:24.270
HARRIS: Some of it.

00:07:24.270 --> 00:07:25.270
Some of it.

00:07:25.270 --> 00:07:30.560
Certainly some cyberattacks that have been
state-sponsored, the goings-on in various

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:34.090
nuclear facilities, we don't consider that
within the realm of what we're talking about.

00:07:34.090 --> 00:07:40.290
But where we see states taking to cyberattacks
against the banking sectors of others countries

00:07:40.290 --> 00:07:44.930
to vent a geopolitical grievance, like we
saw in the early days of the Russia-Georgia

00:07:44.930 --> 00:07:50.830
conflict in 2008, where some of Russia's opening
shots were cyberattacks aimed at the Georgian

00:07:50.830 --> 00:07:54.180
banking sector, that is very much within the
realm of what we're looking at.

00:07:54.180 --> 00:07:59.140
LINDSAY: I take it from your opening remarks,
Bob, that you came to the conclusion that

00:07:59.140 --> 00:08:03.140
some states are good at geoeconomics and some
states aren't.

00:08:03.140 --> 00:08:04.690
Who's really good at it?

00:08:04.690 --> 00:08:06.370
BLACKWILL: China.

00:08:06.370 --> 00:08:10.120
You'd have to start with China.

00:08:10.120 --> 00:08:16.980
And as we discuss in the book at some length,
this isn't a perfect scorecard.

00:08:16.980 --> 00:08:19.770
China doesn't always do it well.

00:08:19.770 --> 00:08:29.870
But for two decades or so, as China became
more powerful, they have begun quite systematically

00:08:29.870 --> 00:08:41.000
to use incentives-interest-free loans-and
disincentives-coercive measures-to affect

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:46.740
the geopolitical positions of other nations.

00:08:46.740 --> 00:08:50.580
Lee Kuan Yew put it like this, which is vivid.

00:08:50.580 --> 00:08:58.940
He said, "the Chinese say publicly all states
are equal.

00:08:58.940 --> 00:09:06.640
But when we do something that they don't like,
they say: have you noticed the size of our

00:09:06.640 --> 00:09:08.750
market?

00:09:08.750 --> 00:09:15.750
And know your place; don't offend 1.2 billion
Chinese."

00:09:15.750 --> 00:09:18.370
So they calibrate this.

00:09:18.370 --> 00:09:26.440
If Japan does something geopolitically they
don't like, Japanese imports of automobiles

00:09:26.440 --> 00:09:28.370
to China diminish.

00:09:28.370 --> 00:09:37.670
If the Philippines don't act in ways in the
South China Sea that Beijing doesn't like,

00:09:37.670 --> 00:09:43.550
Philippine fruits and vegetables rot on the-on
the docks of Chinese ports.

00:09:43.550 --> 00:09:44.930
And so forth.

00:09:44.930 --> 00:09:57.630
So I think they, with this enormous wealth
they now have to use, have been the most skillful-not

00:09:57.630 --> 00:10:08.840
perfect, but skillful-in reminding other countries
that there is an economic price to pay for

00:10:08.840 --> 00:10:12.560
geopolitical behavior that China doesn't like.

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:16.650
LINDSAY: So, Jen, who else makes the list
of countries that are good at geoeconomics?

00:10:16.650 --> 00:10:22.370
HARRIS: Certainly, as Bob mentioned, China
tops that list, but China's not alone.

00:10:22.370 --> 00:10:26.400
You know, Russia's pipeline politics I'm sure
is well-known to all of you.

00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:33.860
But we also see Russia slapping very questionable
sanctions on Ukrainian chocolates that happen

00:10:33.860 --> 00:10:39.360
to be owned by Petro Poroshenko months, in
fact, before we saw protester really fill

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:40.620
the Maidan Square.

00:10:40.620 --> 00:10:50.180
And we also see this-you know, the Gulf countries
in 2015 alone spend some $12 billion in the

00:10:50.180 --> 00:10:53.029
contest for favor with the new Egypt.

00:10:53.029 --> 00:10:58.650
Just, what, last week we saw the Saudis threaten
to dump U.S. Treasury bills if, in fact, Congress

00:10:58.650 --> 00:11:03.680
goes through with pinning liability for 9/11
on the Saudis.

00:11:03.680 --> 00:11:09.000
So certainly, you know, China is a leading
practitioner, but by no means all there is.

00:11:09.000 --> 00:11:11.320
There's ample examples from India, Brazil,
on through as well.

00:11:11.320 --> 00:11:12.350
LINDSAY: OK.

00:11:12.350 --> 00:11:15.630
Two words I haven't heard: United States.

00:11:15.630 --> 00:11:21.710
So is that because the United States is just
mediocre at the practice of geoeconomics,

00:11:21.710 --> 00:11:25.160
or is it bad at it?

00:11:25.160 --> 00:11:26.160
BLACKWILL: You take this one, Jen.

00:11:26.160 --> 00:11:27.160
HARRIS: (Chuckles.)

00:11:27.160 --> 00:11:30.310
Well, I think it's really a forgotten or a
lost art in American statecraft.

00:11:30.310 --> 00:11:34.690
And digging back through the history, what
actually was some of my favorite research

00:11:34.690 --> 00:11:39.780
for the book-as someone who ran from history
in grad school and college, it's sort of cosmic

00:11:39.780 --> 00:11:44.990
irony that this would prove to be some of
the most interesting, anecdote-filled pieces

00:11:44.990 --> 00:11:51.250
of the project-but you really see for the-about
the first 200 years of American history, you

00:11:51.250 --> 00:11:57.020
know, U.S. leaders quite comfortable flexing
U.S. economic muscle in pursuit of explicitly

00:11:57.020 --> 00:12:00.340
geopolitical aims.

00:12:00.340 --> 00:12:04.250
There certainly were-you know, the high-water
marks are pretty well-known to all of us:

00:12:04.250 --> 00:12:06.070
the Marshall Plan.

00:12:06.070 --> 00:12:08.740
But even, you know, things like the Louisiana
Purchase.

00:12:08.740 --> 00:12:13.860
As much as Jefferson liked a good deal, his
main motivation was keeping the French from

00:12:13.860 --> 00:12:19.690
gaining a foothold on the American continent,
and potentially setting a pretty young and

00:12:19.690 --> 00:12:24.050
exhausted U.S. Army up for a confrontation
with Napoleon's troops that we probably wouldn't

00:12:24.050 --> 00:12:25.050
win.

00:12:25.050 --> 00:12:29.530
Even, you know-and some of the-some of the
pieces that are in some ways familiar, but

00:12:29.530 --> 00:12:33.850
yet not, of U.S. geoeconomic statecraft, Lend-Lease
comes to mind.

00:12:33.850 --> 00:12:39.430
Churchill once called this a declaration of
economic war, except it was one that he felt

00:12:39.430 --> 00:12:43.730
was aimed as much at Britain as Berlin, and
he wasn't unfounded in this.

00:12:43.730 --> 00:12:50.290
You know, the terms of what was really about
$660 billion worth of American aid that we

00:12:50.290 --> 00:12:53.760
were lending to our allies in World War II
came at some cost.

00:12:53.760 --> 00:12:59.900
We were unilaterally controlling the level
of British gold, and, you know, unilaterally

00:12:59.900 --> 00:13:05.150
controlling British exports, and seeking to
rewrite the terms of the postwar order with

00:13:05.150 --> 00:13:06.500
the U.S. interests at the center.

00:13:06.500 --> 00:13:08.279
And none of this was lost on the British.

00:13:08.279 --> 00:13:10.280
LINDSAY: I think a lot of people-
BLACKWILL: Could I just chime in.

00:13:10.280 --> 00:13:19.420
One more sense the British are at issue here,
two more.

00:13:19.420 --> 00:13:28.230
One, during the American Civil War, when the
British were tempted to be sympathetic to

00:13:28.230 --> 00:13:33.930
the Confederacy because of their dependence
on Southern cotton, the government-the U.S.

00:13:33.930 --> 00:13:39.320
government, the Lincoln government-told them
that if they wish to have all of their assets

00:13:39.320 --> 00:13:44.080
in the United States expropriated, this would
be a good way to do it.

00:13:44.080 --> 00:13:52.380
And then, of course, Suez comes to mind, in
which the United States-the Eisenhower administration-so

00:13:52.380 --> 00:13:59.050
appalled by the British-French invasion of
Suez, threatened basically to destroy the

00:13:59.050 --> 00:14:02.030
British pound if they proceeded.

00:14:02.030 --> 00:14:11.550
And that was the end of the intervention that
the British were-it wasn't Eisenhower and

00:14:11.550 --> 00:14:21.000
Dulles' general opposition to this, it was
that Harold Macmillan, who was at Number 11-he

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:28.060
was the finance minister to Anthony Eden-said
they can do it, and they'll do it.

00:14:28.060 --> 00:14:33.020
And Eisenhower would have done it, because
Ben-Gurion lied to him in writing about the

00:14:33.020 --> 00:14:34.920
Israeli involvement in this.

00:14:34.920 --> 00:14:38.810
So we used to know how to do it.

00:14:38.810 --> 00:14:44.261
HARRIS: And none of these are things, right,
that we can imagine our friends in the administration,

00:14:44.261 --> 00:14:48.130
on either side of the aisle, Republican or
Democrat, really contemplating with any seriousness

00:14:48.130 --> 00:14:49.130
today.

00:14:49.130 --> 00:14:51.130
LINDSAY: But I would imagine a lot of people
hearing this story would say, well, wait a

00:14:51.130 --> 00:14:57.030
second, the United States resorts to financial
sanctions, trade sanctions quite regularly.

00:14:57.030 --> 00:15:01.250
They were used to try to bring the Iranians
to the negotiating table.

00:15:01.250 --> 00:15:06.890
We applied them against Russia in the wake
of the Russian seizure of Crimea.

00:15:06.890 --> 00:15:10.830
So do those not count as geoeconomics?

00:15:10.830 --> 00:15:12.530
Or is it geoeconomics done badly?

00:15:12.530 --> 00:15:16.620
HARRIS: So we try to be pretty upfront in
the book that we see sanctions as kind of

00:15:16.620 --> 00:15:19.100
the exception that proves the rule.

00:15:19.100 --> 00:15:24.000
Right now, the Obama administration has about
30 sanctions programs in place, which is more

00:15:24.000 --> 00:15:29.010
than any other president in history, I think
more than several combined.

00:15:29.010 --> 00:15:35.000
And it's not just a story about innovation
and quantity, these are really sanctions that

00:15:35.000 --> 00:15:36.570
are different in kind.

00:15:36.570 --> 00:15:44.470
But even as you have seen, you know, real
sort of progress made in the sanctions realm,

00:15:44.470 --> 00:15:50.940
you don't see it encased within a broader
diplomatic strategy that, for instance, managed

00:15:50.940 --> 00:15:56.430
to get the French to turn off military shipments
to Russia sooner than the seven months of

00:15:56.430 --> 00:15:58.940
long, hard conversations that it took.

00:15:58.940 --> 00:16:06.260
Or, you know, conversations within NATO about
what are the proper responsibilities and shared

00:16:06.260 --> 00:16:12.050
understandings are around what is owed to
military allies when it comes to economic

00:16:12.050 --> 00:16:13.050
coercion.

00:16:13.050 --> 00:16:14.930
LINDSAY: What about something like the Trans-Pacific
Partnership?

00:16:14.930 --> 00:16:20.850
There's been a lot of talk about TPP not being
solely about trade, but about trying to set

00:16:20.850 --> 00:16:24.500
the geopolitical order in East Asia.

00:16:24.500 --> 00:16:29.649
Is this an indication of geoeconomics getting
back on the U.S. agenda, or do you think it's

00:16:29.649 --> 00:16:31.110
more symbolism than reality?

00:16:31.110 --> 00:16:34.710
BLACKWILL: Well, it isn't symbolism, but it's
a matter of sequence.

00:16:34.710 --> 00:16:38.300
And we go into it in the book in some detail.

00:16:38.300 --> 00:16:46.880
Our analysis-and there's lots of documentation
available which supports this-is that we do

00:16:46.880 --> 00:16:53.920
not negotiate trade agreements with geopolitical
objectives in mind.

00:16:53.920 --> 00:16:56.220
We simply don't.

00:16:56.220 --> 00:17:02.970
These are trade agreements negotiated by our
experts on trade, which of course are seeking

00:17:02.970 --> 00:17:08.970
to reinforce U.S. national interests, and
make America wealthier, and open up the global

00:17:08.970 --> 00:17:11.369
economy, and so forth.

00:17:11.369 --> 00:17:17.339
But they are negotiated solely on trade criteria.

00:17:17.339 --> 00:17:24.319
After they are agreed, and when administrations
have to sell these agreements to the U.S.

00:17:24.319 --> 00:17:31.289
Congress, they suddenly recognize and assert
their geopolitical importance.

00:17:31.289 --> 00:17:43.840
Ash Carter says that the TPP is worth one
aircraft carrier, OK?

00:17:43.840 --> 00:17:47.999
Colin Powell made similar comments about NAFTA.

00:17:47.999 --> 00:17:52.840
These are all made after the fact, when we're
trying to persuade the Congress-administrations

00:17:52.840 --> 00:17:58.409
are trying to persuade the Congress to approve
them.

00:17:58.409 --> 00:18:06.960
To put it differently, if you did imagine
that there should be geopolitical objectives

00:18:06.960 --> 00:18:17.100
in the TPP, in the agreement, of course you
would address Chinese coercive pressure on

00:18:17.100 --> 00:18:19.159
the nations of Asia.

00:18:19.159 --> 00:18:26.600
Of course you would have that in the agreement,
because it's persistent and it's been relatively

00:18:26.600 --> 00:18:28.809
effective in many cases.

00:18:28.809 --> 00:18:31.799
There's not a word about that.

00:18:31.799 --> 00:18:40.220
And what it demonstrates, at least in our
view, is that these able, dedicated trade

00:18:40.220 --> 00:18:48.190
people don't consider that as an important
element in their negotiation, and there's

00:18:48.190 --> 00:18:53.379
no one there who makes that case.

00:18:53.379 --> 00:18:58.799
And then we do-just to finish, we look at
the history of the bureaucratic politics of

00:18:58.799 --> 00:19:00.239
this.

00:19:00.239 --> 00:19:07.980
And the story which Jen, who was in, as our
chairman said, the Clinton-or the Obama administration

00:19:07.980 --> 00:19:13.130
for Hillary Clinton, working at the State
Department, the bureaucratic politics over

00:19:13.130 --> 00:19:21.690
these decades has reduced the role of the
State Department in these trade negotiations,

00:19:21.690 --> 00:19:24.720
partly at the insistence of the Congress.

00:19:24.720 --> 00:19:29.960
So they're basically far at the margin of
this today.

00:19:29.960 --> 00:19:40.160
So, negotiate them with no geoeconomic content
and sell them with a geoeconomic/geopolitical

00:19:40.160 --> 00:19:42.080
argument is the history of these agreements.

00:19:42.080 --> 00:19:47.520
LINDSAY: So, Jen, I take it that the clock
is running down on the administration you

00:19:47.520 --> 00:19:49.029
worked in.

00:19:49.029 --> 00:19:50.749
Next January we're going to get a new president.

00:19:50.749 --> 00:19:55.580
Leaving aside whoever is going to win the
race, from your vantage point, what should

00:19:55.580 --> 00:19:59.179
the next administration do if it were serious
about geoeconomics?

00:19:59.179 --> 00:20:00.950
What would that look like?

00:20:00.950 --> 00:20:07.950
HARRIS: So Bob and I sketch out a 20-point
prescription agenda in the final chapters

00:20:07.950 --> 00:20:11.200
of the book, which I will not recount right
now.

00:20:11.200 --> 00:20:12.200
(Laughs.)

00:20:12.200 --> 00:20:17.559
In part because I would hope that all of you
agree with us, but reasonable minds can and

00:20:17.559 --> 00:20:23.110
should and will differ on, you know, the particular
tacks to take.

00:20:23.110 --> 00:20:26.399
And a lot of this is going to be fact-bound.

00:20:26.399 --> 00:20:34.049
Our project is really less to argue a case
for what to think about the geoeconomics rules

00:20:34.049 --> 00:20:38.500
and more to lay out a framework for how to
think about them.

00:20:38.500 --> 00:20:47.289
And so, to me, any prescriptive agenda would
have-whatever the particulars of it-would

00:20:47.289 --> 00:20:49.600
have four basic ingredients.

00:20:49.600 --> 00:20:56.340
One, I think you do need a common reference
point, a common conceptual framework with

00:20:56.340 --> 00:21:02.480
a clear definition that allows us to be clear
with ourselves, bureaucratically, and clear

00:21:02.480 --> 00:21:03.480
with our allies.

00:21:03.480 --> 00:21:08.450
In order to call out geoeconomic coercion
when it's happening to our allies in Asia,

00:21:08.450 --> 00:21:10.510
we need to know what it looks like.

00:21:10.510 --> 00:21:12.929
And so I think that's point one.

00:21:12.929 --> 00:21:20.390
Point two would be to really begin to work
this into the bloodstream of some of our alliances.

00:21:20.390 --> 00:21:23.509
Our relationship with Europe is a great example.

00:21:23.509 --> 00:21:28.309
You know, NATO is the closest military alliance
probably the modern world has ever known,

00:21:28.309 --> 00:21:36.289
and yet we remain one of four or five countries
that has not moved beyond very basic most-favored-nation

00:21:36.289 --> 00:21:38.870
status with the EU in trade terms.

00:21:38.870 --> 00:21:44.210
We lack any economic counterpart for what
NATO represents on the security side, even

00:21:44.210 --> 00:21:49.610
as it's very clear that when Russia wants
to, you know, flex geopolitical muscle, it

00:21:49.610 --> 00:21:55.129
is reaching for these economic tools, as we've-as
has been put on pretty clear display in Ukraine.

00:21:55.129 --> 00:22:01.529
So I would want to see us begin to prioritize,
you know, certain geoeconomic responses within

00:22:01.529 --> 00:22:05.110
some of our closest sort of security relationships.

00:22:05.110 --> 00:22:06.440
Third, resources.

00:22:06.440 --> 00:22:10.799
You know, this in part a dollars and-
LINDSAY: Money.

00:22:10.799 --> 00:22:16.980
HARRIS: This is, in part, a dollars-and-cents
issue; $713 million-one of my favorite numbers

00:22:16.980 --> 00:22:22.600
that I have been quoting a lot these days-Monday
through Wednesday in Afghanistan.

00:22:22.600 --> 00:22:28.830
And so when it comes to arguing, you know,
for what we could do for Ukraine in terms

00:22:28.830 --> 00:22:35.700
of giving that country a viable economic foundation
or how we might begin to steel our allies

00:22:35.700 --> 00:22:40.080
in Asia against the kind of geoeconomic coercion
that Bob is talking about, our entire aid

00:22:40.080 --> 00:22:45.869
envelope for Ukraine last year, I believe,
was about $340 million-less than half of Monday

00:22:45.869 --> 00:22:47.880
through Wednesday in Afghanistan.

00:22:47.880 --> 00:22:49.690
So it is, in part, a dollars-and-cents issue.

00:22:49.690 --> 00:22:54.080
But it's also about how this money is authorized.

00:22:54.080 --> 00:23:00.440
When I was in the State Department, we actually
put out a pretty good geoeconomic theory of

00:23:00.440 --> 00:23:06.029
the case in the president's first and I believe
only response to the Arab Spring: his May

00:23:06.029 --> 00:23:12.869
2011 address, which really took full on the
revolutions across the Middle East.

00:23:12.869 --> 00:23:14.769
Forwarded a pretty economic theory of the
case.

00:23:14.769 --> 00:23:16.239
It was loan guarantees.

00:23:16.239 --> 00:23:18.460
It was a billion-dollar debt swap for Egypt.

00:23:18.460 --> 00:23:23.009
It was a set of enterprise funds for Tunisia
and Egypt that were modeled after what we

00:23:23.009 --> 00:23:26.149
had done pretty successfully after the Soviet
Union.

00:23:26.149 --> 00:23:30.239
Three years later, when I left the State Department,
those enterprise funds had yet to cut their

00:23:30.239 --> 00:23:31.239
first check.

00:23:31.239 --> 00:23:35.200
We were still arguing with ourselves, as an
administration, about how to spend this billion-dollar

00:23:35.200 --> 00:23:39.210
debt swap, even as our Egyptian counterparts
in terms of who we were negotiating with had

00:23:39.210 --> 00:23:40.210
since revolved.

00:23:40.210 --> 00:23:43.119
And, you know, events have basically just
outpaced us.

00:23:43.119 --> 00:23:46.799
So we-you know, we need the ability and the
wherewithal from Congress to be able to move

00:23:46.799 --> 00:23:50.359
a little bit more quickly in how we spend
this money, separate and apart from how much

00:23:50.359 --> 00:23:51.359
money we have to spend.

00:23:51.359 --> 00:23:55.049
LINDSAY: Bob, anything you want to add to
that in terms of guidance to the next administration?

00:23:55.049 --> 00:24:00.880
BLACKWILL: I guess-well, they're not-they're
not calling me all that often to ask.

00:24:00.880 --> 00:24:01.880
(Laughter.)

00:24:01.880 --> 00:24:10.389
But I'll just make one point, which Jen and
I emphasize in the book, which is that beginning

00:24:10.389 --> 00:24:18.580
to use these economic tools for geopolitical
purposes is not a replacement for American

00:24:18.580 --> 00:24:29.210
military might, which has to remain the most
stabilizing force in world order, in my opinion.

00:24:29.210 --> 00:24:39.489
However, what we do argue is that we've become
too instinctively dependent on that instrument.

00:24:39.489 --> 00:24:46.200
And one example of that-and I don't know if
there's anybody here who works on the Hill

00:24:46.200 --> 00:24:52.610
or is watching who works on the Hill, but
have a look at what the hearings are on the

00:24:52.610 --> 00:24:59.320
Hill and try to find a hearing that looks
at economic issues in the Foreign Relations

00:24:59.320 --> 00:25:00.320
Committee.

00:25:00.320 --> 00:25:06.159
They look at almost solely security questions.

00:25:06.159 --> 00:25:10.809
This is not to argue, of course, they shouldn't
be looking at security questions.

00:25:10.809 --> 00:25:19.659
But they don't think of economic means worth
exploring in public hearings to accomplish

00:25:19.659 --> 00:25:29.179
some of our strategic objectives in the Middle
East, other than, as you said, Chairman, sanctions.

00:25:29.179 --> 00:25:31.029
We understand what sanctions do.

00:25:31.029 --> 00:25:33.960
We're very good at it in many ways.

00:25:33.960 --> 00:25:38.440
But beyond sanctions, they simply don't think
in that way.

00:25:38.440 --> 00:25:41.649
And what-you mentioned the next administration.

00:25:41.649 --> 00:25:52.519
What we would like the next administration
to do is address this American asset-we have

00:25:52.519 --> 00:25:58.941
the biggest economy in the world, with this
enormous economic power-address this asset

00:25:58.941 --> 00:26:08.960
systematically; and think internally, how
do they organize themselves that, in NSC meetings-we've

00:26:08.960 --> 00:26:16.580
all been in up here-that it actually comes
up, because usually the people who might address

00:26:16.580 --> 00:26:19.529
that aren't in the room.

00:26:19.529 --> 00:26:20.970
Aren't in the room.

00:26:20.970 --> 00:26:25.919
So that's our dream, I guess is the word.

00:26:25.919 --> 00:26:27.229
LINDSAY: Well, fair enough.

00:26:27.229 --> 00:26:30.720
What I want to do now is bring our members
into the conversation.

00:26:30.720 --> 00:26:35.840
Let me remind everybody that this meeting
is on the record.

00:26:35.840 --> 00:26:39.980
I will also ask you to wait for the microphone
and speak directly into it.

00:26:39.980 --> 00:26:45.460
I would also ask that you please stand, state
your name and affiliation.

00:26:45.460 --> 00:26:51.450
And if people would keep their questions concise,
we can get everybody a chance to talk on this

00:26:51.450 --> 00:26:53.019
provocative topic, we'll do that.

00:26:53.019 --> 00:26:55.090
Let's start over here.

00:26:55.090 --> 00:26:57.460
And Sam is going to come with a microphone
right behind you.

00:26:57.460 --> 00:26:58.960
Q: Oh, you're over here, OK.

00:26:58.960 --> 00:26:59.960
Snuck up on me.

00:26:59.960 --> 00:27:00.960
Hi.

00:27:00.960 --> 00:27:04.399
John Sullivan with George Mason University,
formerly with the Center for International

00:27:04.399 --> 00:27:06.100
Private Enterprise.

00:27:06.100 --> 00:27:10.289
Do you include U.S. foreign assistance in
your range of instruments that you look at?

00:27:10.289 --> 00:27:14.669
It would seem to me logical to do it, not
that U.S. foreign assistance is often used

00:27:14.669 --> 00:27:15.669
strategically.

00:27:15.669 --> 00:27:16.769
But it could be.

00:27:16.769 --> 00:27:22.539
HARRIS: Yes, we do, both economic and military.

00:27:22.539 --> 00:27:25.019
Military is among the least interesting of
the bunch.

00:27:25.019 --> 00:27:29.169
Only there because money's fungible, so military
assistance that we are giving is freeing up

00:27:29.169 --> 00:27:33.499
budget space for the Egyptians or others that
they put to other ends.

00:27:33.499 --> 00:27:40.200
And, yeah, I think we do need to have a real
conversation about the use of economic assistance

00:27:40.200 --> 00:27:46.119
as a foreign policy tool, and what constitutes
an acceptable versus unacceptable use of,

00:27:46.119 --> 00:27:48.299
you know, U.S. aid.

00:27:48.299 --> 00:27:52.249
We also need to have a conversation about
how the world has changed, and what new tools

00:27:52.249 --> 00:27:54.619
in the assistance space we should be considering.

00:27:54.619 --> 00:28:00.419
I, for one, would like to see the U.S. begin
to look at instruments that would allow for

00:28:00.419 --> 00:28:03.849
things like OPIC to take equity positions
in addition to debt.

00:28:03.849 --> 00:28:10.049
That is a thing that's pretty routine and
happens as a regular course across our European

00:28:10.049 --> 00:28:14.179
counterparts, not to mention the likes of
what the Chinese state-led policy banks are

00:28:14.179 --> 00:28:19.609
throwing out at-you know, their going-out
strategies across Latin America and Africa.

00:28:19.609 --> 00:28:20.609
That's something.

00:28:20.609 --> 00:28:24.099
BLACKWILL: Can I just chime in and say-and
Jen mentioned Egypt earlier.

00:28:24.099 --> 00:28:31.250
I like this question because it allows me
to give you this one, which is not rhetorical.

00:28:31.250 --> 00:28:38.789
So what would you think will have the biggest
influence on the future of the Middle East:

00:28:38.789 --> 00:28:45.950
the war in Yemen or the future of the Egyptian
economy?

00:28:45.950 --> 00:28:48.750
And I think most of us would know the answer
to that.

00:28:48.750 --> 00:28:55.950
And yet, we do pitifully little on the future
of the Egyptian economy.

00:28:55.950 --> 00:29:05.279
Now, this isn't to suggest that economy is
our responsibility or that we can fix it.

00:29:05.279 --> 00:29:15.869
But imagine a NSC meeting in which the subject
was how can we use American economic tools

00:29:15.869 --> 00:29:23.480
comprehensively to bolster the economic future
of Egypt.

00:29:23.480 --> 00:29:31.359
And I posit that that economic future will
be more decisive regarding American vital

00:29:31.359 --> 00:29:42.470
national interests than most of what preoccupies
the administration and its predecessors, and

00:29:42.470 --> 00:29:43.499
the media.

00:29:43.499 --> 00:29:47.629
LINDSAY: Let's go over here to Professor Hollifield.

00:29:47.629 --> 00:29:53.749
Q: Jim Hollifield, this year at the Wilson
Center, normally at the Tower Center at SMU

00:29:53.749 --> 00:29:55.379
in Dallas.

00:29:55.379 --> 00:30:02.019
How do you take into account sort of conventional
macroeconomic policy-exchange-rate policy,

00:30:02.019 --> 00:30:03.019
interest-rate policy?

00:30:03.019 --> 00:30:08.259
I mean, the U.S. does control the world's
reserve currency.

00:30:08.259 --> 00:30:10.759
So how do you think about Bretton Woods?

00:30:10.759 --> 00:30:17.840
And secondly, if we have stopped doing this-I
think we did it, certainly, in the '50s and

00:30:17.840 --> 00:30:20.730
'60s-stopped doing this, why did we stop doing
it?

00:30:20.730 --> 00:30:25.190
BLACKWILL: I think the question-I'm going
to turn this one over to Jen about because

00:30:25.190 --> 00:30:29.730
it's-I think it's a terrific question.

00:30:29.730 --> 00:30:33.960
The back half we really have struggled a lot
with, about.

00:30:33.960 --> 00:30:35.679
We think we know what happened.

00:30:35.679 --> 00:30:37.809
Why did it happen?

00:30:37.809 --> 00:30:44.669
And we got into this a little bit on the trade
side, saying that the Congress and successive

00:30:44.669 --> 00:30:52.379
administrations basically took any strategic
perspective out of the trade negotiations

00:30:52.379 --> 00:30:56.879
through bureaucratic politics by having a
trade representative, taking it out of the

00:30:56.879 --> 00:30:59.470
State Department, and so forth.

00:30:59.470 --> 00:31:01.469
But over to Jen.

00:31:01.469 --> 00:31:03.889
HARRIS: It's almost overdetermined.

00:31:03.889 --> 00:31:10.480
So we trace this story about when the U.S.
sort of abandoned, you know, economics as

00:31:10.480 --> 00:31:15.029
a first resort, and why, to about the end
of the Cold War, around Vietnam.

00:31:15.029 --> 00:31:17.749
A couple things were going on at that time,
right?

00:31:17.749 --> 00:31:22.940
One, there are-there's some bureaucratic politics,
as Bob mentioned-the USTR being pulled out

00:31:22.940 --> 00:31:25.690
of the State Department and given its own
independent mandate.

00:31:25.690 --> 00:31:30.599
It's also the first generation of economic
insecurity the country had experienced since

00:31:30.599 --> 00:31:31.599
the Great Depression.

00:31:31.599 --> 00:31:35.869
It was no longer clear that we had economic
carrots to be spending about on our geopolitical

00:31:35.869 --> 00:31:36.869
agendas.

00:31:36.869 --> 00:31:41.580
It was also the rise of the multinational
corporation, complete with a lobbying presence

00:31:41.580 --> 00:31:45.289
here in Washington, that began to change some
of the discourse in Congress.

00:31:45.289 --> 00:31:50.299
And you see that, you know, line up very well
in terms of the Congressional Record.

00:31:50.299 --> 00:31:57.889
But to be a little provocative, I think one
theory about a lesser-appreciated factor has

00:31:57.889 --> 00:32:01.860
more to do, actually, with what's going on
in the discipline of economics than foreign

00:32:01.860 --> 00:32:02.860
policy.

00:32:02.860 --> 00:32:08.349
Namely, it was the sort of-right the moment
when Keynes is going out of fashion and Milton

00:32:08.349 --> 00:32:09.349
Friedman was coming into fashion.

00:32:09.349 --> 00:32:15.720
And we happened to be up against an adversary,
in the Soviet Union, who made no particular

00:32:15.720 --> 00:32:21.289
love of markets and had no particular use
for trade, and so every win for the United

00:32:21.289 --> 00:32:25.679
States in terms of freeing trade and liberalizing
markets was also a win for us in geopolitical

00:32:25.679 --> 00:32:26.759
terms.

00:32:26.759 --> 00:32:31.049
And so perhaps it was no accident, actually,
that you saw this happy convergence between

00:32:31.049 --> 00:32:36.359
the U.S.'s geopolitical interests and the
neoliberal economic orthodoxy that we began

00:32:36.359 --> 00:32:38.499
seeing kind of rising toward its own.

00:32:38.499 --> 00:32:40.299
And it worked pretty well for a while.

00:32:40.299 --> 00:32:45.539
I think in the early years after the Cold
War, we faced no great adversary that required

00:32:45.539 --> 00:32:48.479
us to rethink whether this happy alignment
still exists.

00:32:48.479 --> 00:32:54.499
But now we're up against countries like China
and Russia that, once again, appear to have

00:32:54.499 --> 00:32:58.470
no particular distinctions between state and
market, and are pretty comfortable exercising

00:32:58.470 --> 00:32:59.659
power in economic terms.

00:32:59.659 --> 00:33:04.330
And I think maybe it's time to ask whether
this happy alignment of our-of our security

00:33:04.330 --> 00:33:07.489
and our economic sort of orthodoxies still
exists.

00:33:07.489 --> 00:33:08.610
LINDSAY: Avis.

00:33:08.610 --> 00:33:12.009
Eric (sp) will bring you a microphone.

00:33:12.009 --> 00:33:18.599
Q: Avis Bohlen, retired Foreign Service officer,
former sometime colleague of Robert's.

00:33:18.599 --> 00:33:24.249
I just-I had a few skeptical thoughts in-
BLACKWILL: Good.

00:33:24.249 --> 00:33:29.700
Q: -reaction to what you said, and also a
question.

00:33:29.700 --> 00:33:32.169
The first point, I think, Jen, you already
answered.

00:33:32.169 --> 00:33:39.429
I was going to say that it's so much in our
ideology about free markets and non-interference.

00:33:39.429 --> 00:33:44.529
And I would also add another point, that the
U.S. government has a lot less control over

00:33:44.529 --> 00:33:48.869
the U.S. economy than a country like China
does over its economy.

00:33:48.869 --> 00:33:53.190
So it just wasn't in our thinking, and not
just since Milton Friedman.

00:33:53.190 --> 00:33:58.720
I mean, you know, we've been pushing free
trade since the Marshall Plan, as you-as you

00:33:58.720 --> 00:33:59.720
mentioned.

00:33:59.720 --> 00:34:09.500
So it's-I really question whether we're-you
know, what's the word?-ideologically set up-set

00:34:09.500 --> 00:34:14.129
up to do this, or materially set up to do
it.

00:34:14.129 --> 00:34:16.730
The second point is the balance of our interests.

00:34:16.730 --> 00:34:22.210
You mentioned the example of the French not
being willing to give up selling the boats

00:34:22.210 --> 00:34:23.210
to Russia.

00:34:23.210 --> 00:34:25.529
Well, I mean, come on, that was tiresome.

00:34:25.529 --> 00:34:28.099
The French are always tiresome, as we know.

00:34:28.099 --> 00:34:29.099
(Laughter.)

00:34:29.099 --> 00:34:30.599
But you know, they're a very important ally.

00:34:30.599 --> 00:34:35.779
We're going to unleash the full power of the
United States economy to achieve this?

00:34:35.779 --> 00:34:41.730
I mean, I think there is always a balance
of interests, and we have a lot of interests

00:34:41.730 --> 00:34:44.200
around the world which are going to really
preclude this.

00:34:44.200 --> 00:34:47.490
Third point is I think-
LINDSAY: Well, Avis, if I may, can I hold

00:34:47.490 --> 00:34:49.059
you there so I let them have a chance to answer?

00:34:49.059 --> 00:34:51.260
We have a lot of questions in the audience
I want to get to.

00:34:51.260 --> 00:34:54.060
Q: Could I just mention that I think USAID
has always been a political instrument, and

00:34:54.060 --> 00:34:57.230
I saw it in Eastern Europe in the-(off mic).

00:34:57.230 --> 00:34:58.630
BLACKWILL: Thank you, Avis.

00:34:58.630 --> 00:34:59.650
Nice to see you.

00:34:59.650 --> 00:35:05.030
For those of you who don't know, Avis is one
of the premier experts on how tiresome the

00:35:05.030 --> 00:35:06.150
French can be.

00:35:06.150 --> 00:35:07.150
(Laughter.)

00:35:07.150 --> 00:35:09.119
Day by day by day.

00:35:09.119 --> 00:35:12.910
Enormous patience and skill on her part.

00:35:12.910 --> 00:35:20.500
We don't suggest in the book that these instruments
be used crudely in every case.

00:35:20.500 --> 00:35:27.279
But what we do say is that administrations,
in the kinds of meetings that you used to

00:35:27.279 --> 00:35:33.079
routinely go to when you were here in policy
positions, is that they be at least raised

00:35:33.079 --> 00:35:36.000
to see if they have potential.

00:35:36.000 --> 00:35:44.710
So we don't want to hit an ant with a sledgehammer,
but we want to use these economic instruments

00:35:44.710 --> 00:35:52.200
the way we routinely-routinely-use security
coercion, or at least imploration.

00:35:52.200 --> 00:35:55.310
So that's that.

00:35:55.310 --> 00:36:05.440
But the second is, more broadly, we used to-and
we have a chapter in the book on the history

00:36:05.440 --> 00:36:15.369
of American use of this-we used to do this
with the people in the room, the counterparts

00:36:15.369 --> 00:36:23.430
of the current Cabinet members, who just as
a matter of course thought this way.

00:36:23.430 --> 00:36:37.329
And among-and I won't mention names here-but
among those who resist our policy prescriptions

00:36:37.329 --> 00:36:44.320
are former secretaries of the Treasury, because,
as those of us who have worked in government

00:36:44.320 --> 00:36:54.350
know, they tend to think of their business
as too important for foreign policy considerations.

00:36:54.350 --> 00:37:00.640
Or the trade rep-I'm not speaking now about
any particular one of them-who tend to think,

00:37:00.640 --> 00:37:04.339
and the Congress in particular tends to say,
oh, I know what these people at the State

00:37:04.339 --> 00:37:12.390
Department are thinking; they want to trade
away American jobs for geopolitical purposes.

00:37:12.390 --> 00:37:15.480
And so we used to know how to do this.

00:37:15.480 --> 00:37:21.400
It wasn't questioned that this-these were
effective tools.

00:37:21.400 --> 00:37:26.410
Sometimes they could be used badly, but that
they were available.

00:37:26.410 --> 00:37:31.770
And that's, at least in our experience-which
covers certainly the period since the end

00:37:31.770 --> 00:37:34.980
of the Cold War-not the case.

00:37:34.980 --> 00:37:40.950
I'll give you one other example, which you'll
be, Avis, very familiar with.

00:37:40.950 --> 00:37:53.799
What's the primary geopolitical action-successful
action in the 1990s?

00:37:53.799 --> 00:38:02.010
It's Helmut Kohl going to Russia and writing
a check for German unification.

00:38:02.010 --> 00:38:09.260
That-what better example of the use of an
economic instrument to pay for the departure

00:38:09.260 --> 00:38:16.900
of the Red Army from East Germany and for
the apartments that were built for them and-a

00:38:16.900 --> 00:38:19.290
beautiful, beautiful example.

00:38:19.290 --> 00:38:26.400
Meanwhile, we were doing-trying to do enterprise
funds for-I was in the administration, in

00:38:26.400 --> 00:38:32.579
the White House-for Eastern European transformation.

00:38:32.579 --> 00:38:33.600
And we had no money.

00:38:33.600 --> 00:38:36.010
So basically it's we had no money.

00:38:36.010 --> 00:38:40.330
So we used to know how to do it, and we hope
we'll learn how to do it again.

00:38:40.330 --> 00:38:42.070
LINDSAY: Mr. Paris.

00:38:42.070 --> 00:38:49.529
Q: Jonathan Paris, former Middle East fellow
here and-I'm a former Middle East fellow and

00:38:49.529 --> 00:38:51.900
currently a London-based analyst.

00:38:51.900 --> 00:38:57.279
Just quickly on Egypt, of course the Egyptian
economy is important, but it'd be nice if

00:38:57.279 --> 00:39:01.740
we could help them keep their planes from
falling out of the sky, because that really

00:39:01.740 --> 00:39:03.480
impacts on their tourism.

00:39:03.480 --> 00:39:05.829
But my question is on China.

00:39:05.829 --> 00:39:09.319
China seems to have a good-a good deal going
in Africa, for instance.

00:39:09.319 --> 00:39:14.010
They get a military base in Djibouti when
they put in $12 billion of infrastructure

00:39:14.010 --> 00:39:15.369
in Ethiopia and Djibouti.

00:39:15.369 --> 00:39:17.240
And what do we get stuck with?

00:39:17.240 --> 00:39:22.440
We get stuck with doing all this counterterrorism
against Boko Haram in Mali.

00:39:22.440 --> 00:39:27.720
So, you know, maybe the problem is they're
getting a free ride on the counterterrorism

00:39:27.720 --> 00:39:32.970
and they get to get these chits, you know,
building ports, and then they get a whole

00:39:32.970 --> 00:39:36.940
bunch of security benefits from all their
infrastructure.

00:39:36.940 --> 00:39:42.569
BLACKWILL: I think Jen leaves for China tonight,
so maybe she should speak to that.

00:39:42.569 --> 00:39:43.569
HARRIS: (Chuckles.)

00:39:43.569 --> 00:39:45.369
Oh, I think you're absolutely right.

00:39:45.369 --> 00:39:50.900
And I think you're beginning to see strands
of this bubble up in the context of the U.S.

00:39:50.900 --> 00:39:51.900
general election.

00:39:51.900 --> 00:39:57.960
It's not too dissimilar from some of the noises
that Donald Trump certainly has made in questioning

00:39:57.960 --> 00:40:00.109
whether our allies are free-riding.

00:40:00.109 --> 00:40:06.690
And China has been masterful in its ability
to parlay either the present reality of its

00:40:06.690 --> 00:40:12.970
economic power or the projections of future
growth, which probably matter just as much,

00:40:12.970 --> 00:40:15.890
into sort of its geopolitical throw-weight.

00:40:15.890 --> 00:40:17.569
And so we could not agree more.

00:40:17.569 --> 00:40:23.119
I think this is-you know, Britain has a project
to try to get-force Washington to begin to

00:40:23.119 --> 00:40:25.480
wake up and confront what are not easy questions.

00:40:25.480 --> 00:40:29.960
Absolutely these tools are, again, not to
be used lightly, nor are they without controversy

00:40:29.960 --> 00:40:30.960
or costs.

00:40:30.960 --> 00:40:31.960
And we try to be quite clear about that.

00:40:31.960 --> 00:40:33.670
The problem is not that they cost something.

00:40:33.670 --> 00:40:34.859
Of course they cost something.

00:40:34.859 --> 00:40:39.440
But so too do every other form of statecraft,
especially our military.

00:40:39.440 --> 00:40:45.640
BLACKWILL: And I'd say I just think it's a
good question about the Chinese in Africa.

00:40:45.640 --> 00:40:54.240
They use the construction of soccer stadiums
as a geopolitical instrument.

00:40:54.240 --> 00:40:58.660
And if you-I think they've now built over
30 of them.

00:40:58.660 --> 00:41:04.270
And they are coincidentally in proximity to
the home villages of the president of the

00:41:04.270 --> 00:41:06.170
country.

00:41:06.170 --> 00:41:10.869
So this is pretty micro, but exactly what
you say.

00:41:10.869 --> 00:41:14.970
Now, they can make mistakes, they can overdo
it, or so forth.

00:41:14.970 --> 00:41:23.069
But look at the-at-which we do in the book-the
connection between Chinese economic assistance

00:41:23.069 --> 00:41:29.369
to Africa and African votes at the U.N. on
issues we care about.

00:41:29.369 --> 00:41:31.829
And-
HARRIS: Or recognition of Taiwan.

00:41:31.829 --> 00:41:38.200
BLACKWILL: -or-yes, so-or recognition of Taiwan,
where there's a direct correlation.

00:41:38.200 --> 00:41:45.400
So that, using assistance for their geopolitical
goals, is routine for them.

00:41:45.400 --> 00:41:47.839
LINDSAY: Go to the back of the room.

00:41:47.839 --> 00:41:49.339
Q: Yes, hi.

00:41:49.339 --> 00:41:50.339
Thank you.

00:41:50.339 --> 00:41:54.170
The name is Mercedes Fitchett with the Department
of Defense.

00:41:54.170 --> 00:42:00.780
Back in 2003 after the Iraq invasion, where
we were building the whole coalition effort,

00:42:00.780 --> 00:42:06.619
we actually used the-over $18 billion in U.S.
free construction projects as a stick and

00:42:06.619 --> 00:42:10.540
a carrot for increasing the number of countries.

00:42:10.540 --> 00:42:15.200
And I recall there was a huge battle with
France in terms of them being able to bid

00:42:15.200 --> 00:42:17.829
on contracts, that they weren't part of the
coalition.

00:42:17.829 --> 00:42:23.130
But did you all happen to take a look at what
we did with that-with that tool in terms of

00:42:23.130 --> 00:42:25.619
growing the coalition in your book as a case
study?

00:42:25.619 --> 00:42:26.619
BLACKWILL: Yeah.

00:42:26.619 --> 00:42:31.880
I was the presidential envoy to Iraq when
you were-when you were working those, which

00:42:31.880 --> 00:42:38.410
was-doesn't entirely explain our failure there
but is a major part of it.

00:42:38.410 --> 00:42:44.160
Let me make this distinction.

00:42:44.160 --> 00:42:50.150
And you were involved in it, so you'll have
much more details and maybe a definitive thought

00:42:50.150 --> 00:42:51.819
different from mine.

00:42:51.819 --> 00:42:58.010
When we were-and I was at the White House-we
were working our way through this, we didn't

00:42:58.010 --> 00:43:04.580
think of those reconstruction efforts-first
of all, it's, of course, total chaos, as you

00:43:04.580 --> 00:43:10.829
know and very little systematic thinking of
any kind in any case.

00:43:10.829 --> 00:43:20.220
But insofar as we did think about it, we did
not think about the long-term future of Iraq

00:43:20.220 --> 00:43:23.869
and how we might spend this money.

00:43:23.869 --> 00:43:28.910
Most of the recommendations that came in that
we spent money in Iraq were from our military

00:43:28.910 --> 00:43:35.520
commanders, who were trying-and of course
it was important that they try-to stabilize

00:43:35.520 --> 00:43:42.109
wherever they were: in the western desert,
up north, and in the south near Basra.

00:43:42.109 --> 00:43:50.630
So we didn't think about it in the kind of
geoeconomic terms that Jen and I discussed

00:43:50.630 --> 00:43:51.630
in the book.

00:43:51.630 --> 00:44:04.380
Secondly-now, I will display a prejudice-our
geoeconomic thinking was infected by the virus

00:44:04.380 --> 00:44:13.920
of "one man and woman, one vote" and, quote,
"election objectives" in both Afghanistan

00:44:13.920 --> 00:44:16.500
and Iraq.

00:44:16.500 --> 00:44:28.210
And we became mesmerized by these election
processes at the expense of geoeconomic thinking.

00:44:28.210 --> 00:44:38.260
So summing up, we spent our money-that money
very badly in-obviously in both those countries,

00:44:38.260 --> 00:44:40.480
very badly.

00:44:40.480 --> 00:44:46.109
And I think one of the reasons was that we
didn't, as Jen was urging and as we urge in

00:44:46.109 --> 00:44:57.940
the book, we didn't think of these as tools
beyond the either pure development in a micro-area

00:44:57.940 --> 00:45:07.700
or tools beyond bringing these people to democracy,
which, as we've seen, would have meant-since

00:45:07.700 --> 00:45:15.359
it failed, of course, completely-would have
meant changing their entire culture and political

00:45:15.359 --> 00:45:17.559
sociology.

00:45:17.559 --> 00:45:22.740
So we didn't-
HARRIS: Just one postscript of optimism, I'll

00:45:22.740 --> 00:45:29.079
have you know, which is that it's also true,
though, that we find that a lot of U.S. military

00:45:29.079 --> 00:45:34.119
leaders are some of the most powerful surrogates
and champions for exactly the argument that

00:45:34.119 --> 00:45:35.119
we're pushing.

00:45:35.119 --> 00:45:41.089
As we describe in the book, when I was in
the early, tough stages of thinking through

00:45:41.089 --> 00:45:47.599
Secretary Clinton's economic statecraft agenda,
I sat down with Admiral Mike Mullen and sort

00:45:47.599 --> 00:45:49.640
of heard him riff on some of this.

00:45:49.640 --> 00:45:54.750
And he gave more powerful expression to some
of these realities than anybody I had met

00:45:54.750 --> 00:45:55.750
before or since.

00:45:55.750 --> 00:46:00.400
And he just said, you know what, compare the
man hours, woman hours we, the U.S. military,

00:46:00.400 --> 00:46:05.510
have spent thinking through the size and composition
of the Afghan National Security Forces to

00:46:05.510 --> 00:46:13.890
the man/woman hours we have spent thinking
through a viable economic blueprint for that

00:46:13.890 --> 00:46:14.890
country's future.

00:46:14.890 --> 00:46:18.150
And until we get that balance closer to where
it should be, I think we should expect to,

00:46:18.150 --> 00:46:20.940
you know, continue paying for it and influence-(inaudible).

00:46:20.940 --> 00:46:31.990
BLACKWILL: Right, and Bob Gates, as you know,
in his book and in his public speeches urges

00:46:31.990 --> 00:46:38.380
much more funds for the State Department for
essentially geoeconomic objectives.

00:46:38.380 --> 00:46:46.069
And Leon Panetta did the same, and Ash Carter
does the same, so far essentially to deaf

00:46:46.069 --> 00:46:47.160
ears in the Congress.

00:46:47.160 --> 00:46:49.740
LINDSAY: OK, we have time for one more question.

00:46:49.740 --> 00:46:54.619
Before I take it, I want to remind everybody
in the room this conversation has been on

00:46:54.619 --> 00:46:55.619
the record.

00:46:55.619 --> 00:47:01.089
I will also let people know that copies of
Bob and Jen's book are available for sale

00:47:01.089 --> 00:47:02.319
at the back of the room.

00:47:02.319 --> 00:47:05.119
Those joining us on Internet can-
BLACKWILL: So lock the doors.

00:47:05.119 --> 00:47:06.119
(Laughter.)

00:47:06.119 --> 00:47:08.530
LINDSAY: -always go on Amazon.com and other
e-retailers.

00:47:08.530 --> 00:47:11.880
With that said, sir, you have the last question.

00:47:11.880 --> 00:47:13.660
Q: Thanks very much.

00:47:13.660 --> 00:47:16.069
Karan Bhatia with General Electric.

00:47:16.069 --> 00:47:19.519
I was wondering if you could comment on a
thesis that I think may be a corollary to

00:47:19.519 --> 00:47:24.380
what you're talking about, which is that the
intentions and the ultimate objectives of

00:47:24.380 --> 00:47:28.940
a number of these other countries you referred
to are in fact more economic in their core.

00:47:28.940 --> 00:47:34.770
So you look at One Belt, One Road, for instance,
in China, or you look at the position that

00:47:34.770 --> 00:47:39.329
a number of European countries have taken
on sanctions, at the end of the day you sort

00:47:39.329 --> 00:47:43.559
of look at those and you say, yeah, there's
a core strong economic element to it, advancing

00:47:43.559 --> 00:47:48.039
their economic interest, whereas in the United
States we tend not to think about foreign

00:47:48.039 --> 00:47:49.660
policy in those kinds of terms.

00:47:49.660 --> 00:47:50.839
Would you agree with that?

00:47:50.839 --> 00:47:56.579
Is that-is that a notion that sort of sits
well alongside the thesis of your book?

00:47:56.579 --> 00:47:57.579
How do you see that idea?

00:47:57.579 --> 00:47:58.579
BLACKWILL: Well, I think that-
Q: (Off-mic)-General Electric-

00:47:58.579 --> 00:47:59.579
LINDSAY: I'm sorry?

00:47:59.579 --> 00:48:07.970
Q: It's another question that I think should
have been asked, is (would ?) General Electric

00:48:07.970 --> 00:48:13.910
have geopolitical ambitions-
LINDSAY: I'd like to hold that for another

00:48:13.910 --> 00:48:14.910
meeting.

00:48:14.910 --> 00:48:17.259
I'd like to give Bob and Jen an opportunity
to wrap up and then-

00:48:17.259 --> 00:48:23.390
BLACKWILL: One Belt, One Road-well, we don't
know for sure, since the Chinese government

00:48:23.390 --> 00:48:27.740
systematically lies about its strategic objectives,
as we know.

00:48:27.740 --> 00:48:34.500
So we don't know for sure, but I do think
it has a strong economic core.

00:48:34.500 --> 00:48:38.250
But I think at its base, it's geopolitical.

00:48:38.250 --> 00:48:43.349
That is to say, if you look at the behavior
in the last 20 years, they systematically

00:48:43.349 --> 00:48:50.500
build up dependencies, which they use for
their geopolitical purposes.

00:48:50.500 --> 00:48:52.490
And they do it again and again and again.

00:48:52.490 --> 00:48:57.900
And they remind people of these dependencies,
nations, when it matters.

00:48:57.900 --> 00:49:00.269
So it's both.

00:49:00.269 --> 00:49:01.750
So it's both.

00:49:01.750 --> 00:49:06.450
But I think if you believe it's only-which
is what the Chinese, of course, say, which

00:49:06.450 --> 00:49:11.770
is One Belt, One Road is only for the benefit
of the people of Asia, period.

00:49:11.770 --> 00:49:18.279
Well, it has that dimension, if it produces
wealth and jobs and so forth.

00:49:18.279 --> 00:49:24.270
But I can tell you I spend quite a bit of
time in Central Asia, and they-the people

00:49:24.270 --> 00:49:29.610
in Central Asia do not take at face-value
that there are no geopolitical Chinese objectives

00:49:29.610 --> 00:49:31.299
in this-in this policy.

00:49:31.299 --> 00:49:33.010
LINDSAY: Any final word, Jen?

00:49:33.010 --> 00:49:37.569
HARRIS: So I think you're-Karan, you're getting
to one of what was, I think, the hardest questions

00:49:37.569 --> 00:49:40.599
for us in the book, which is, how do you know
it when you see it?

00:49:40.599 --> 00:49:44.509
Countries rarely-sometimes they do helpfully
signpost pretty explicitly like the Saudis

00:49:44.509 --> 00:49:51.200
did last week or as the Chinese have done
in conditioning certain investments by sovereign

00:49:51.200 --> 00:49:54.619
wealth funds on Costa Rica's disavowal of
Taiwan.

00:49:54.619 --> 00:49:56.460
There are pretty smoking-gun cases.

00:49:56.460 --> 00:50:00.299
But more often and usually in the cases of
where it's most effective, it's much more

00:50:00.299 --> 00:50:03.970
the stuff of correlation than direct causation.

00:50:03.970 --> 00:50:09.240
And so it-but it's also the case that just
because there is some economic-you know, presence

00:50:09.240 --> 00:50:12.570
of economic rationale doesn't negate also
the present of geopolitical rationale.

00:50:12.570 --> 00:50:16.680
You know, all the better if there are twofers
and threefers, I think, for the Chinese.

00:50:16.680 --> 00:50:21.460
But there are certain cases where, you know,
deals just don't make economic sense, and

00:50:21.460 --> 00:50:22.460
yet they happen anyway.

00:50:22.460 --> 00:50:27.789
And even in some of the IPOs that Chinese
state-owned enterprises have come into, you

00:50:27.789 --> 00:50:31.859
know, New York and listed on our stock exchanges,
when you actually go through and read the

00:50:31.859 --> 00:50:36.859
securities filings-I have done that-not the-you
know, the most fun weekend I've ever spent,

00:50:36.859 --> 00:50:41.859
but they're very up-front about the fact that
these are companies that are owned by the

00:50:41.859 --> 00:50:46.150
Chinese government, that there are certain
objectives that are political that will occasionally

00:50:46.150 --> 00:50:50.119
trump-it's all kind of there black-and-white
when you sort of know what you're looking

00:50:50.119 --> 00:50:51.119
for.

00:50:51.119 --> 00:50:56.130
So, you know, I think there are certain cases
where you are going to just have to, you know,

00:50:56.130 --> 00:50:58.310
initiate some kind of a smell test.

00:50:58.310 --> 00:51:04.320
But just the sheer presence of economic motivation
doesn't negate, you know, the co-presence

00:51:04.320 --> 00:51:05.320
of geopolitical rationales.

00:51:05.320 --> 00:51:07.119
I just wanted to also add-
BLACKWILL: Can I just-

00:51:07.119 --> 00:51:09.259
LINDSAY: I will-I will give the ambassador-I'll
give you the final word.

00:51:09.259 --> 00:51:13.710
BLACKWILL: Well, no, no, just one last one,
which we can-another good example from China,

00:51:13.710 --> 00:51:24.079
which is in this category Jen was mentioning,
notice the eerie coincidence that in the-in

00:51:24.079 --> 00:51:32.349
the preliminaries to the visit of a Chinese
president to the United States for a summit

00:51:32.349 --> 00:51:41.920
with our president, notice the eerie coincidence
of major Chinese purchases of Boeing aircraft.

00:51:41.920 --> 00:51:45.210
They just do this routinely, and it's not
just Boeing.

00:51:45.210 --> 00:51:46.349
It's Brazilian aircraft.

00:51:46.349 --> 00:51:48.930
We go into lots of examples.

00:51:48.930 --> 00:51:56.300
And I can assure you that in our-in the deliberations
of our highest councils of government, these

00:51:56.300 --> 00:52:03.089
sorts of notions-and of course the private
sector for us, so it's not completely analogous-but

00:52:03.089 --> 00:52:10.700
just thinking through what do we-what economic
instruments do we have in order to deal with

00:52:10.700 --> 00:52:18.830
the rise of Chinese power or to deal with
China-sorry, Russia and their use, of course,

00:52:18.830 --> 00:52:26.559
of pipeline politics, or to help foster our
national interests in the Middle East, what

00:52:26.559 --> 00:52:27.559
are they?

00:52:27.559 --> 00:52:29.460
And they just don't come up in the conversation.

00:52:29.460 --> 00:52:35.000
LINDSAY: That is going to have to be the final
word on a very complex, timely, and deep subject.

00:52:35.000 --> 00:52:40.049
However, the good news for everybody in the
room is that you can talk to Jen and Bob as

00:52:40.049 --> 00:52:41.049
we wrap up.

00:52:41.049 --> 00:52:45.460
And of course all of you are more than welcome
and encouraged to buy a copy of "War by Other

00:52:45.460 --> 00:52:46.460
Means."

00:52:46.460 --> 00:52:48.630
So please join me in thanking Bob and Jen
for a stimulating conversation.

00:52:48.630 --> 00:52:49.630
(Applause.)

00:52:49.630 --> 00:52:50.630
HARRIS: Thank you.

00:52:50.630 --> 00:52:50.631
(END)

